<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samsenthai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1373–<a href="/years/1416">1416</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Oun Heuan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samsenthai, born Oun Heuan, was the second king of Lan Xang, ruling from 1373 to 1416 after the nobles deposed his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fa-ngum">Fa Ngum</a>. His regnal name, &quot;three hundred thousand Tai&quot;, is traditionally linked to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of his male subjects. His long and peaceful reign consolidated the administration and Buddhist institutions of the kingdom.</p></div>
